00:40:56,730 --> 00:41:03,050
f prime and then f
prime squared minus

500
00:41:03,050 --> 00:41:07,940
and then f f double
prime, like that.

501
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:13,220
That's the quotient for
taking the derivative.

502
00:41:13,220 --> 00:41:15,510
And so you see that
you can write this--

503
00:41:15,510 --> 00:41:17,910
if you split this up into two--

504
00:41:17,910 --> 00:41:19,700
this here is one.

505
00:41:19,700 --> 00:41:21,030
You split it up to two.

506
00:41:21,030 --> 00:41:26,270
This here is f prime squared
divided by f prime squared.

507
00:41:26,270 --> 00:41:28,700
And then this here becomes plus.

508
00:41:28,700 --> 00:41:34,250
And then I'm writing
it as f f double prime

509
00:41:34,250 --> 00:41:38,480
over [INAUDIBLE] squared.

510
00:41:38,480 --> 00:41:41,880
And so you see that this
thing here cancel with that.

511
00:41:41,880 --> 00:41:43,550
So these here cancel.

512
00:41:43,550 --> 00:41:48,260
So you have that the
derivative of this map

513
00:41:48,260 --> 00:41:56,990
is just f of x, f prime,
double prime of x, f prime

514
00:41:56,990 --> 00:41:59,095
of x, and then squared.

515
00:41:59,095 --> 00:42:02,210
That's the derivative.

516
00:42:02,210 --> 00:42:17,840
And now, of course, if I'm using
the mean value theorem here,

517
00:42:17,840 --> 00:42:22,080
then I have that
the derivative--

518
00:42:27,830 --> 00:42:33,500
then I have that T
of x minus T of y--

519
00:42:33,500 --> 00:42:37,100
the mean value theorem
say that this thing here

520
00:42:37,100 --> 00:42:41,240
is the derivative.

521
00:42:41,240 --> 00:42:43,530
It's actually Wallis
theorem or whatever.

522
00:42:43,530 --> 00:42:46,250
But you can also think about
this mean value theorem.

523
00:42:46,250 --> 00:42:51,800
It's a derivative at somewhere
in between x and y times

524
00:42:51,800 --> 00:42:53,665
the difference between x and y.

525
00:42:55,990 --> 00:42:57,240
That's the mean value theorem.

526
00:43:02,360 --> 00:43:07,520
So you see that if we already
knew-- so if we can show--

527
00:43:07,520 --> 00:43:17,850
if we knew that the derivative
was bounded everywhere,

528
00:43:17,850 --> 00:43:22,670
was bounded by some c, little
c that was less than 1--

529
00:43:22,670 --> 00:43:25,350
if you had that,
then, of course,

530
00:43:25,350 --> 00:43:27,720
then you just take the
absolute value of this.

531
00:43:27,720 --> 00:43:31,820
So then you have that
T of x minus T of y,

532
00:43:31,820 --> 00:43:36,140
the absolute value
was equal to T

533
00:43:36,140 --> 00:43:41,090
prime in this intermediate
point, x minus y.

534
00:43:41,090 --> 00:43:43,130
But in this intermediate
point, we're

535
00:43:43,130 --> 00:43:45,230
assuming that everywhere
in the derivative

536
00:43:45,230 --> 00:43:46,860
is less than or equal to c.

537
00:43:46,860 --> 00:43:52,040
So now this is less than or
equal to c, so c x minus y.

538
00:43:52,040 --> 00:43:55,160
And we're assuming that c
is strictly less than 1.

539
00:43:55,160 --> 00:43:59,740
So you see that this here, if
we knew that the derivative of T

540
00:43:59,740 --> 00:44:02,690
was strictly less
than 1 everywhere,

541
00:44:02,690 --> 00:44:08,200
then we would know that this
map is a contracting map.

542
00:44:08,200 --> 00:44:08,780
OK.

543
00:44:08,780 --> 00:44:12,880
But, of course, what we
have for the derivative is,

544
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:14,265
we have this in here.

545
00:44:19,420 --> 00:44:24,150
But now the idea is that if you
start with a really good guess--

546
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:42,375
so the idea with [INAUDIBLE]
is, start with a good guess.

547
00:44:49,300 --> 00:44:53,920
And then I'm not going
to make that too precise,

548
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:55,700
as I'm putting it
in quotation marks.

549
00:44:55,700 --> 00:44:59,600
But the idea is
that you want to--

550
00:44:59,600 --> 00:45:00,650
so good guesses.

551
00:45:00,650 --> 00:45:05,650
Remember that what we want is,
we're interested in this point

552
00:45:05,650 --> 00:45:09,400
here, where this function here--

553
00:45:09,400 --> 00:45:13,426
we're interested in the point
where the function vanishes.

554
00:45:13,426 --> 00:45:16,000
So starting with
a good guess means

555
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:20,690
that you may not start
where the function vanishes,

556
00:45:20,690 --> 00:45:24,800
but you're starting somewhere
where the value is really small.

557
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:27,980
That would be a good guess.

558
00:45:27,980 --> 00:45:38,770
And so start with a guess,
meaning that f at that point

559
00:45:38,770 --> 00:45:43,365
is small, is very small.

560
00:45:45,880 --> 00:45:47,380
Now, of course,
you're going to ask,

561
00:45:47,380 --> 00:45:49,570
what do you mean by very small?

562
00:45:49,570 --> 00:45:55,600
So very small here is going
to mean that the value--

563
00:45:55,600 --> 00:45:58,990
so now we have, from this here--

564
00:45:58,990 --> 00:46:05,110
so we have that the
derivative here of this map

565
00:46:05,110 --> 00:46:10,160
here was equal to-- these
two terms cancel out.

566
00:46:10,160 --> 00:46:14,230
So this is f of
x, f double prime

567
00:46:14,230 --> 00:46:20,260
of x, f prime of x squared.

568
00:46:20,260 --> 00:46:24,370
So starting with a good guess
means that this thing here

569
00:46:24,370 --> 00:46:26,180
is really, really small.

570
00:46:26,180 --> 00:46:29,630
And, in fact, I want it to
be small compared with this.

571
00:46:29,630 --> 00:46:32,990
So if this here is really
small compared with that,

572
00:46:32,990 --> 00:46:35,830
then I can guarantee that
the absolute value of this

573
00:46:35,830 --> 00:46:38,626
is smaller than 1.

574
00:46:38,626 --> 00:46:42,370
And so you see that if you
start with a really good guess,

575
00:46:42,370 --> 00:46:45,730
then it would be a
contracting mapping.

576
00:46:45,730 --> 00:46:49,100
And then, of course,
you need to prove that.

577
00:46:49,100 --> 00:46:51,230
So you're forming the sequence.

578
00:46:51,230 --> 00:46:53,500
And you need to prove--

579
00:46:53,500 --> 00:46:56,260
somehow, you need to
estimate, that you

580
00:46:56,260 --> 00:47:01,240
need to be able to say that
once you form the sequence,

581
00:47:01,240 --> 00:47:03,730
the next thing that
it's spitting out

582
00:47:03,730 --> 00:47:07,420
is also a good guess,
so that you stay

583
00:47:07,420 --> 00:47:09,170
in this range of good guess.

584
00:47:09,170 --> 00:47:12,970
And if it has this
property, then it

585
00:47:12,970 --> 00:47:15,400
would be a contracting
map, and you would

586
00:47:15,400 --> 00:47:17,110
guarantee to get a fixed point.

587
00:47:17,110 --> 00:47:21,230
But that's all the
Newton methods say.

588
00:47:21,230 --> 00:47:27,260
So you need to justify why, if
you start with a good guess,

589
00:47:27,260 --> 00:47:30,170
why, as you're
iterating this process,

590
00:47:30,170 --> 00:47:34,780
why you keep in the
range of good guesses.

591
00:47:34,780 --> 00:47:40,610
But the Newton method doesn't
tell you anything about that.

592
00:47:40,610 --> 00:47:43,455
But it require
that to be applied.

593
00:47:46,730 --> 00:47:47,230
OK.

594
00:47:47,230 --> 00:47:48,880
So it's a contracting map.

595
00:47:48,880 --> 00:47:52,390
This T is a contracting map.

596
00:47:52,390 --> 00:47:54,790
If I can start
with a good guess--

597
00:47:54,790 --> 00:47:57,300
and when I do this,
when I form-- so I

598
00:47:57,300 --> 00:47:59,080
start with this good guess.

599
00:47:59,080 --> 00:48:04,440
And then I'm looking at that
the next thing is this T of x

600
00:48:04,440 --> 00:48:08,010
and then a T squared of x.

601
00:48:08,010 --> 00:48:11,710
By then, I want all of these
to also be good guesses.

602
00:48:11,710 --> 00:48:15,900
And then I'm saying that
as n goes to infinity,

603
00:48:15,900 --> 00:48:20,770
this here is constant, is going
to converge to this fixed point.

604
00:48:20,770 --> 00:48:22,890
I guess I called it x0.

605
00:48:22,890 --> 00:48:34,890
But this is the fixed
point, fixed point for T.

606
00:48:34,890 --> 00:48:39,130
So now we started
with a function.

607
00:48:39,130 --> 00:48:44,760
And what we wanted to
find a root, so at x0,

608
00:48:44,760 --> 00:48:47,216
where this function vanishes.

609
00:48:47,216 --> 00:48:50,680
And I constructed
a contracting map.

610
00:48:50,680 --> 00:48:54,810
But now I will need to
relate the fixed point

611
00:48:54,810 --> 00:48:57,780
for the contracting map
to the original problem.

612
00:48:57,780 --> 00:49:02,280
So what is a fixed point
for the contracting map?

613
00:49:02,280 --> 00:49:07,590
So the map here,
the map we defined

614
00:49:07,590 --> 00:49:15,365
was T of x is equal to x minus
f of x over f prime of x.

615
00:49:17,970 --> 00:49:20,320
But a fixed point--

616
00:49:20,320 --> 00:49:24,810
so if x0 is a fixed
point, then this

617
00:49:24,810 --> 00:49:32,070
means that this here is the
same as x0 minus f of x0, f

618
00:49:32,070 --> 00:49:34,440
prime of x0.

619
00:49:34,440 --> 00:49:37,300
And now you can just
write-- so therefore,

620
00:49:37,300 --> 00:49:45,210
this implies that f of x0 minus
f of x0 over f prime of x0, this

621
00:49:45,210 --> 00:49:45,870
here.

622
00:49:45,870 --> 00:49:48,430
Then you would subtract
x0 0 on both sides.

623
00:49:48,430 --> 00:49:50,520
And this is equal to 0.

624
00:49:50,520 --> 00:49:53,710
But that, of course, means
the denominator must be 0.

625
00:49:56,400 --> 00:50:02,880
So a fixed point is indeed
a point where f of x at 0

626
00:50:02,880 --> 00:50:04,150
is equal to 0.

627
00:50:07,785 --> 00:50:08,285
OK.

628
00:50:12,090 --> 00:50:17,850
So now we want to prove the
Cauchy convergence theorem.

629
00:50:17,850 --> 00:50:20,370
But we're going to cheat
just a little bit, because we

630
00:50:20,370 --> 00:50:23,640
are going to deduce it
from another theorem

631
00:50:23,640 --> 00:50:26,790
that I will prove next time.

632
00:50:26,790 --> 00:50:34,920
So to prove the
Cauchy convergence

633
00:50:34,920 --> 00:50:38,760
for the Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem--

634
00:50:38,760 --> 00:50:41,020
and that theorem,
we'll prove next time.

635
00:50:48,300 --> 00:50:49,685
So what is the Bolzano--

636
00:51:05,550 --> 00:51:07,870
so the Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem.

637
00:51:07,870 --> 00:51:09,630
So the Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem

638
00:51:09,630 --> 00:51:30,480
says that every bounded sequence
has a convergent subsequence.

639
00:51:34,920 --> 00:51:36,940
So that's a Bolzano-Weierstrass.

640
00:51:39,852 --> 00:51:42,330
Now let's try to--

641
00:51:42,330 --> 00:51:43,570
we'll assume this.

642
00:51:43,570 --> 00:51:46,470
Again, I'll prove
this next time.

643
00:51:46,470 --> 00:51:51,510
But let's use this theorem to
prove the Cauchy convergence

644
00:51:51,510 --> 00:51:53,630
theorem.

645
00:51:53,630 --> 00:52:11,750
So from the
Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem,

646
00:52:11,750 --> 00:52:28,470
we get the Cauchy convergence
theorem as follows.

647
00:52:35,480 --> 00:52:38,090
So, remember, the Cauchy
convergence theorem

648
00:52:38,090 --> 00:52:42,260
say that if you have a Cauchy
sequence, then it is convergent.

649
00:52:42,260 --> 00:52:56,060
So suppose xn this
[? position, ?] OK?

650
00:52:56,060 --> 00:52:59,000
I want to prove that
it's convergent.

651
00:52:59,000 --> 00:53:10,320
But we already shown that
any Cauchy sequence--

652
00:53:16,400 --> 00:53:20,950
shown that xn, any Cauchy
sequence is bounded.

653
00:53:24,950 --> 00:53:27,222
This is this [INAUDIBLE].

654
00:53:31,410 --> 00:53:44,990
Therefore, by the
Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem,

655
00:53:44,990 --> 00:53:51,600
there's a convergent
subsequence.

656
00:53:59,090 --> 00:54:01,820
So there's a convergent
subsequent xnk.

657
00:54:23,690 --> 00:54:32,600
So you have that
this subsequence

658
00:54:32,600 --> 00:54:37,250
here converge to some x.

659
00:54:37,250 --> 00:54:42,200
And now I want to show that the
original sequence converge to x.

660
00:54:46,630 --> 00:55:02,800
So since xn is a
Cauchy sequence,

661
00:55:02,800 --> 00:55:14,600
given epsilon greater than
0, there exists a capital N

662
00:55:14,600 --> 00:55:20,500
so that if m and n are
bigger than capital N,

663
00:55:20,500 --> 00:55:28,990
then xn minus xm is less
than epsilon over 2.

664
00:55:28,990 --> 00:55:33,250
I'm using the epsilon
in the definition

665
00:55:33,250 --> 00:55:34,640
of being a Cauchy sequence.

666
00:55:37,780 --> 00:55:41,145
And let's call this-- this
is the N. Let's called it N1.

667
00:55:44,590 --> 00:55:59,860
So now since xnk converge
to x, but that epsilon,

668
00:55:59,860 --> 00:56:15,190
there exist N2 so that if
k here is bigger than N2,

669
00:56:15,190 --> 00:56:23,470
then xnk minus x is less
than epsilon over 2.

670
00:56:45,640 --> 00:57:04,491
So now we take n to be equal
to the maximum of N1 and nN2.

671
00:57:07,690 --> 00:57:10,730
This is the one that
corresponds to y.

672
00:57:10,730 --> 00:57:14,680
So this is where k is
taking equal to this N2.

673
00:57:17,830 --> 00:57:25,510
So if we take n like that and
you're looking at n bigger equal

674
00:57:25,510 --> 00:57:29,860
to N and you now want to
estimate this thing here,

675
00:57:29,860 --> 00:57:43,480
then you can think about this
difference here as x minus xn,

676
00:57:43,480 --> 00:58:02,655
like this, plus x, xN minus
xn [INAUDIBLE] like that.

677
00:58:10,190 --> 00:58:14,090
So this is now in
this subsequent.

678
00:58:14,090 --> 00:58:18,290
And so one of them-- this
is less than epsilon over 2.

679
00:58:18,290 --> 00:58:21,400
And this here is less
than epsilon over 2.

680
00:58:21,400 --> 00:58:23,350
And so you get that
this difference here

681
00:58:23,350 --> 00:58:24,280
was less than epsilon.

682
00:58:28,780 --> 00:58:32,770
So this is why the
Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem

683
00:58:32,770 --> 00:58:37,400
implies the Cauchy
convergence theorem.

684
00:58:37,400 --> 00:58:37,900
OK.

685
00:58:37,900 --> 00:58:41,280
So now the next
thing is that I want

686
00:58:41,280 --> 00:58:48,360
to give another application of
the Cauchy convergence here.

687
00:58:48,360 --> 00:58:52,360
So here's another application of
the Cauchy convergence theorem.

688
00:59:08,250 --> 00:59:12,070
So one application was to the
contracting mapping theorem.

689
00:59:12,070 --> 00:59:17,180
But here's another application
of the Cauchy convergence

690
00:59:17,180 --> 00:59:17,680
theorem.

691
00:59:26,880 --> 00:59:29,260
So this is the
extreme value theorem.

692
00:59:38,700 --> 00:59:47,430
Now, before I
explain that, let me

693
00:59:47,430 --> 00:59:51,690
introduce this notion
of a function being

694
00:59:51,690 --> 00:59:56,340
continuous in a rigorous way.

695
00:59:56,340 --> 01:00:02,010
So what does it mean for a
function to be continuous?

696
01:00:02,010 --> 01:00:13,882
So a function f, say, from R
to R could be a subset of R,

697
01:00:13,882 --> 01:00:31,320
could be all of R, is said to
be continuous at some point x0

698
01:00:31,320 --> 01:00:39,090
if, for all epsilon
greater than 0,

699
01:00:39,090 --> 01:00:53,280
there exist delta greater
than 0 such that if x minus x0

700
01:00:53,280 --> 01:00:58,860
is smaller than delta,
then the images are

701
01:00:58,860 --> 01:01:01,985
within epsilon region.

702
01:01:06,210 --> 01:01:08,910
So if you think about it--

703
01:01:08,910 --> 01:01:13,890
so if you have the function--
this is a function.

704
01:01:13,890 --> 01:01:20,190
You should say that it's
continuous at some x0, means

705
01:01:20,190 --> 01:01:23,520
that if this thing here is--

706
01:01:23,520 --> 01:01:27,390
this here is now f of x0.

707
01:01:27,390 --> 01:01:29,850
If you take a little
interval around here--

708
01:01:29,850 --> 01:01:32,060
this is a epsilon interval--

709
01:01:32,060 --> 01:01:36,530
around here, then there
should be some delta interval

710
01:01:36,530 --> 01:01:39,710
around here so that if you're
looking at images here,

711
01:01:39,710 --> 01:01:43,140
then it will map into here.

712
01:01:43,140 --> 01:01:46,895
That's what it means
to be continuous.

713
01:01:46,895 --> 01:01:50,240
OK, so this is
continuous at a point.

714
01:01:50,240 --> 01:01:53,270
And a function is
said to be continuous

715
01:01:53,270 --> 01:01:55,300
if it is continuous
at all points.

716
01:01:59,780 --> 01:02:23,300
So a function is
said to be continuous

717
01:02:23,300 --> 01:02:33,730
if it is continuous
at all points.

718
01:02:42,050 --> 01:02:45,890
And so now we have a
little theorem here.

719
01:02:45,890 --> 01:02:53,000
And this is often useful way
of thinking about continuity.

720
01:02:53,000 --> 01:02:58,590
So if f is continuous--

721
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:05,430
so let's just say it
doesn't need to be all of R,

722
01:03:05,430 --> 01:03:08,030
but you just have to be careful
that everything that you

723
01:03:08,030 --> 01:03:09,930
talk about is in the domain.

724
01:03:09,930 --> 01:03:13,550
But let's just say that f
is a function from R to R,

725
01:03:13,550 --> 01:03:14,626
is continuous.

726
01:03:19,880 --> 01:03:29,960
So if f is continuous and xn
is a sequence that converges

727
01:03:29,960 --> 01:03:35,900
to x, then the images--

728
01:03:35,900 --> 01:03:38,460
so f of xn--

729
01:03:38,460 --> 01:03:40,340
this is another sequence--

730
01:03:40,340 --> 01:03:42,480
those converge to f of x.

731
01:03:46,580 --> 01:03:51,200
This is a useful way of
thinking about being continuous.

732
01:03:53,810 --> 01:03:56,830
And so now, why
is this the case?

733
01:04:01,280 --> 01:04:13,826
So proof-- since
f is continuous--

734
01:04:18,380 --> 01:04:22,670
you only need that is
continuous at each x.

735
01:04:22,670 --> 01:04:25,490
So since it's
continuous at x, this

736
01:04:25,490 --> 01:04:28,585
means that for all
epsilon that are given--

737
01:04:31,100 --> 01:04:34,280
given epsilon
greater than 0, there

738
01:04:34,280 --> 01:04:50,210
exist delta greater than
0 such that if x minus y--

739
01:04:50,210 --> 01:04:52,970
this is this x
here that you think

740
01:04:52,970 --> 01:04:56,660
about-- is fixed, if you
have another point that

741
01:04:56,660 --> 01:05:08,540
is within delta, then the images
f of x minus f of y [INAUDIBLE].

